rname>Su</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hasui</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Sueyoshi</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Matsushita</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsuyama</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>B. S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>J. B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Higashi</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Yonezawa</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Murata</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Expression of mucins in the human fetal and neonatal stomach</article-title><source>Acta Histochem. Cytochem.</source><volume>37</volume><fpage>163</fpage><lpage>172</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B47"><label>47</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tak</surname><given-names>P. P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Bresnihan</surname><given-names>B.</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>The pathogenesis and prevention of joint damage in rheumatoid arthritis</article-title><source>Arthritis Rheum.</source><volume>43</volume><fpage>2619</fpage><lpage>2633</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11145019</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B48"><label>48</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tatematsu</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsukamoto</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Inada</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Stem cells and gastric cancer: role of gastric and intestinal mixed intestinal metaplasia</article-title><source>Cancer Sci.</source><volume>94</volume><fpage>135</fpage><lpage>141</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12708487</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B49"><label>49</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tatematsu</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Tsukamoto</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mizoshita</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Role of Helicobacter pylori in gastric carcinogenesis: the origin of gastric cancers and heterotopic proliferative glands in Mongolian gerbils</article-title><source>Helicobacter</source><volume>10</volume><fpage>97</fpage><lpage>106</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15810939</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B50"><label>50</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weissman</surname><given-names>I. L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>D. J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gage</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Stem and progenitor cells: origin, phenotypes, lineage commitments, and transdifferentiation</article-title><source>Annu. Rev. Cell Dev. Biol.</source><volume>17</volume><fpage>387</fpage><lpage>403</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11687494</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B51"><label>51</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Whitfield</surname><given-names>M. L.</given-names></name><name><surname>George</surname><given-names>L. K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Grant</surname><given-names>G. D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Perou</surname><given-names>C. M.</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Common markers of proliferation</article-title><source>Nat. Rev. Cancer</source><volume>6</volume><fpage>99</fpage><lpage>106</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16491069</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B52"><label>52</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Willingham</surname><given-names>M. C.</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Cytochemical methods for the detection of apoptosis</article-title><source>J. Histochem. Cytochem.</source><volume>47</volume><fpage>1101</fpage><lpage>1109</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10449530</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B53"><label>53</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wotherspoon</surname><given-names>A. C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ortiz-Hidalgo</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Falzon</surname><given-names>M. R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Isaacson</surname><given-names>P. G.</given-names></name></person-group><year>1991</year><article-title>Helicobacter pylori-associated gastritis and primary B-cell gastric lymphoma</article-title><source>Lancet</source><volume>8776</volume><fpage>1175</fpage><lpage>1176</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1682595</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;1</label><caption><p>Histogenesis of a human adult gastric fundic gland from viewpoints of the stem cells and the pathogenicity of HP infection. <bold>a)</bold> Conceptual relationship among stem cells in a gastric fundic gland in each developmental stage. Embryonic stem cells grow to become mature cells through progenitor cells and precursor cells (blasts). In stomach, mature cells reveal growth with functional development, maintaining a two-layered structure of the gastric fundic gland, and finally show homeostatic growth under homeostatic mass control in the adult. The primitive fundic gland is a simple tubular gland and comprises the blasts. After organogenesis the fetal fundic gland is a ramified tubular gland and comprises the mature cells. After birth the fundic glands continue the growth with functional development and reach to the stage of the adult gland with homeostatic growth. In the adult fundic glands, mature cells include gastric epithelial stem cells, proliferating mature cells and terminally differentiated cells. In the same manner, the other organs and tissues develop and some of them, such as bone marrow and adipose tissue, include their tissue (pluripotent) stem cells. In stomach, tissue stem cells (gastric stem cells) can differentiate to gastric epithelial stem cells. However, the relationship among these tissue (pluripotent) stem cells and the peripheral blood stem cells has yet to be elucidated. On the other hand, induced pluripotent stem cells from mature cells suggests the unique idea that stem cell phenotype can be acquired. Gastric fundic glandular cells begin to proliferate bidirectionally at the site of stem cells. In the pipe-line system, foveolar epithelial cells are differentiated and die after their terminal differentiation and their life span. In the stochastic flow system, fundic glandular cells are differentiated and die after their terminal differentiation and their life span. <bold>b)</bold> The pathogenicity of HP infection in gastric mucosa. HP infection directly and indirectly effects the homeostatic growth of the gastric mature cells and the gastric mucosal immunity. The effects on the latter contribute to persistent inflammation, appearance of reactive lymphoid hyperplasia and MALT-type lymphomagenesis. One of the effects on the former is explained to be due to CagA protein that suppresses apoptotic loss of surface foveolar epithelial cells and promotes persistent infection of HP. The effects on gastric epithelial stem cells in fundic glands have yet to be clearly elucidated.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f01a"/><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f01b"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;2</label><caption><p>Mostly normal-appeared mucosa taken from the resected stomach with cancer (H&#x00026;E stain, &#x000d7;10 magnification). <bold>a)</bold> Mostly normal gastric fundic mucosa. <bold>b)</bold> Inflamed gastric fundic mucosa with HP infection. Reactive lymphoid hyperplasia (developed mucosal associated lymphoid apparatus) is noted. <bold>c)</bold> Inflamed intestinal metaplastic gastric mucosa. There were obvious intestinal type glands with appearance of Paneth&#x02019;s cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f02"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;3</label><caption><p>Proliferating cells labeled by hAR-polymer method of Ki67 antigen (&#x000d7;10 magnification). <bold>a)</bold> Mostly normal gastric fundic mucosa. Some labeled proliferating cells located in the neck portion. <bold>b)</bold> Inflamed gastric mucosa with HP infection. Many labeled proliferating cells were seen in the elongated neck portion. Some proliferating cells were also noted in the fundic glandular areas. In the stroma with chronic inflammatory cell infiltration, there were some Ki7 antigen-positive proliferating cells. In a germinal center of the lymph follicle there were many Ki67 antigen-positive proliferating cells. <bold>c)</bold> Intestinal metaplasia. The metaplastic glands demonstrated labeled proliferating cells in the base portion of the glands.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f03"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;4</label><caption><p>Immunostaining of CD117, CD34 and CD133 for stem cells in the bone marrow and in the peripheral blood (&#x000d7;40 magnification). <bold>a&#x02013;d)</bold> CD34. <bold>e</bold> and <bold>f)</bold> CD117. <bold>g</bold> and <bold>h)</bold> CD133. <bold>a, b, e, f</bold> and <bold>g)</bold> Aspiration clot specimen of the bone marrow. <bold>c, d</bold> and <bold>h)</bold> Peripheral blood tissue specimen (PBTS). <bold>a, c</bold> and <bold>e)</bold> hAR-polymer method. <bold>b, d, f, g</bold> and <bold>h)</bold> hAR-supersensitive method. In the bone marrow, beside CD34-positive blood vessel endothelia, immunostaining by hAR-polymer method of CD34 showed more positive cells than that of CD117, and immunostaining of hAR-supersensitive method showed more and stronger positive cells (<bold>h</bold>) in the order of CD117 (<bold>f</bold>), CD34 (<bold>b</bold>) and CD133 (<bold>g</bold>). In the peripheral blood, a few CD34-positive cells (<bold>c, d</bold>) and CD133-positive cells were noted.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f04"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;5</label><caption><p>Immunostaining of CD117, CD34 and CD133 for stem cells in the gastric mucosa. <bold>a&#x02013;d)</bold> CD117. <bold>a)</bold> Inflamed mucosa (hAR-polymer method, &#x000d7;40 magnification). CD117-positive cells were noted in and around the neck portion and in the stroma with dense chronic infiltrates. <bold>b)</bold> Inflamed mucosa (hAR-supersensitive method, &#x000d7;40 magnification). A trace expression of CD117 in gastric glandular cells at the neck portion. <bold>c)</bold> Fundic glands in inflamed mucosa (hAR-polymer method, &#x000d7;40 magnification). Some CD117-positive fundic glandular cells under the neck portion. <bold>d)</bold> Intestinal metaplasia (hAR-polymer method, &#x000d7;40 magnification). CD117-positive cells in the base portion of a metaplastic gland. <bold>e&#x02013;g)</bold> CD34. <bold>e)</bold> Inflamed mucosa (hAR-supersensitive method, &#x000d7;20 magnification). Besides blood vessel endothelia, CD34-positive fat cells and glandular cells were seen but most fundic glandular cells were labeled very weakly. <bold>f)</bold> Fundic glands in the inflamed mucosa (hAR-supersensitive method, &#x000d7;20 magnification). Fundic glandular cells were labeled by CD34. <bold>g)</bold> Intestinal metaplasia (hAR-supersensitive method, &#x000d7;10 magnification). Residual fundic glandular cells in metaplastic mucosa were also labeled by CD34. <bold>h)</bold> CD133. Mostly normal mucosa (hAR-supersensitive method, &#x000d7;10 magnification, long axis: 680 &#x000b5;m). Fundic glands were labeled densely by CD133.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f05"/></fig><fig id="F6" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;6</label><caption><p>Immunostaining of muramidase and of CD68 (hAR-polymer method, &#x000d7;10 magnification). <bold>a)</bold> Muramidase in the mostly normal mucosa. Muramidase was noted in glandular cells from the surface to the neck portion. <bold>b)</bold> Muramidase in inflamed mucosa. Muramidase was also noted in deep fundic glandular cells. <bold>c)</bold> CD68 in an area of intestinal metaplasia. There were CD68-positive cells in the stroma and CD68-positive metaplastic glandular cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f06"/></fig><fig id="F7" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;7</label><caption><p>Immunostaining of single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) and cleaved caspase-3 for apoptosis in the gastric mucosa. <bold>a)</bold> eAR-polymer method of ssDNA in the mostly normal gastric mucosa (&#x000d7;20 magnification). Anti-ssDNA antibody labeled most of the nuclei of the glandular cells. <bold>b)</bold> hAR-polymer method of cleaved caspase-3 in the mostly normal gastric mucosa (&#x000d7;20 magnification). Foveolar cells under the surface were positive for cleaved caspase-3. <bold>c)</bold> hAR-supersensitive method of cleaved caspase-3 in the mostly normal gastric mucosa (&#x000d7;20 magnification). Labeled cells other than those labeled by hAR-polymer method (<bold>b</bold>) were thought to degrade the prepared caspase-3. <bold>d)</bold> hAR-polymer method of cleaved caspase-3 in inflamed mucosa (&#x000d7;20 magnification). Relatively many cleaved caspase-3-positive foveolar cells under the eroded surface. Some cleaved caspase-3-positive cells were also seen in the neck portion. <bold>e)</bold> hAR-polymer method of cleaved caspase-3 in the neck portion of the inflamed mucosa (&#x000d7;20 magnification). Some cleaved caspase-3-positive cells in the neck portion where many proliferation cells were located. <bold>f)</bold> hAR-polymer method of cleaved caspase-3 in intestinal metaplasia (&#x000d7;20 magnification). Surface cells of the metaplastic glands were positive for cleaved caspase-3.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f07"/></fig><fig id="F8" position="float"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;8</label><caption><p>Supersensitive immunostaining of beclin-1 in the mostly normal and inflamed gastric mucosa. <bold>a)</bold> eAR-supersensitive method (&#x000d7;10 magnification). A beclin-1-positive nucleus was noted in the inserted figure (&#x000d7;20 objective and &#x000d7;6.4 digital enlargement, length of axis was 67 &#x000b5;m). <bold>b)</bold> hAR-supersensitive method (&#x000d7;10 magnification). Foveolar cells were weakly labeled and fundic glandular cells were densely stained. <bold>c)</bold> eAR-supersensitive method (&#x000d7;40 magnification). A fundic gland included some beclin-1-positive cells and some of them were degenerated. <bold>d)</bold> eAR-supersensitive method (&#x000d7;40 magnification). In fundic glands under the neck portion some nuclei were positive for beclin-1. <bold>e)</bold> eAR-supersensitive method (&#x000d7;40 magnification). In fundic glands under the neck portion, an atrophic gland showed glandular cells which revealed cytoplasmic and nuclear staining of beclin-1.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AHC07021f08"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table&#x000a0;1</label><caption><p>Cases and epithelial lesions examined</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Case</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mucosa</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflammation</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Glandular lesion examined</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fundic (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">2</xref>a)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rare lymphocytes in thin interglandular stroma</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal 1</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">B</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fundic</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rare lymphocytes in thin interglandular stroma</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal 2</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="2" align="center" valign="top" colspan="1">C</td><td rowspan="2" align="left" valign="top" colspan="1">Fundic</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some lymphocytes in thin interglandular stroma<hr/></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 1<hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Moderate infiltration of dominantly lymphocytes in parts in the upper portion of the mucosa. Tiny lymph follicles in the base portion of the mucosa.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 2</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fundic</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Moderate infiltration of dominantly lymphocytes in the upper portion of the mucosa. Tiny lymph follicle formation in the base portion of the mucosa.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 3</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">E</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fundic (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">2</xref>b)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sever infiltration of dominantly lymphocytes in the upper portion of the mucosa. Marked lymph follicle formation in the lower and base portion of the mucosa.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 4</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">F</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fundic</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sever infiltration of dominantly lymphocytes in the upper portion of the mucosa. Marked lymph follicle formation in the lower and base portion of the mucosa.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 5</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td rowspan="4" align="center" valign="top" colspan="1">G</td><td rowspan="2" align="left" valign="top" colspan="1">Metaplastic with residual fundic glands (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">2</xref>c)</td><td rowspan="2" align="left" valign="top" colspan="1">Moderate infiltration of dominantly lymphocytes in the upper and lower portion of the mucosa with focal completely intestinal metaplasia.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Residual 1<hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic 1</td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some infiltration of dominantly lymphocytes in thin interglandular stroma in the mucosa with completely intestinal metaplasia.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic 2</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table&#x000a0;2</label><caption><p>Antibodies and antigen retrieval methods employed in this study</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th colspan="3" align="center" rowspan="1">Antibody<hr/></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th colspan="2" align="center" rowspan="1">Antigen retrieval<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Name</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Catalog No.</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dilution</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Method</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Solution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ki67 antigen (MIB-1)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">M7240, Dako</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01 M citrate buffer pH 6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CD34 (QBEnd 10)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">M7165, Dako</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01 M citrate buffer pH 6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CD117</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NCL-CD117, Novocastra Lab. Ltd.</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:40</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EDTA solution</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CD133</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">431133, clone 170411, Techne Co.</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01 M citrate buffer pH 8</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">muramidase</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A0099, Dako</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:500</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01 M citrate buffer pH 6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CD68</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">PG-M1, M0876, Dako</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01 M citrate buffer pH 6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">single-stranded DNA (ssDNA)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A4506, Dako</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:100</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Enzymatic treatment</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">200 &#x000b5;g/ml proteinase K</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">cleaved caspase-3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5A1, Asp175, Cell Signaling Co.</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:200</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">EDTA solution</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">beclin-1 (H-300)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sc-11427, Santa Cruz Biotech. Inc.</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1:50</td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Autoclave</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01 M citrate buffer pH 8</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Enzymatic treatment</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">200 &#x000b5;g/ml proteinase K</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>Antibody-Dilution</p><p>The antibody dilution solution was Antibody Diluent (ChemMate, S2022, Dako) in the case of the polymer method and 2% Bovine serum albumin (BSA)-0.1% tween 20-0.01 M phosphate buffer saline pH 7.2 (PBS) in the case of the supersensitive immunohistochemistry.</p><p>Antigen retrieval-Method</p><p>Autoclave: Heated by means of autoclave at 121&#x002da;C, for 5 min, and cooled to room temperature.</p><p>Enzymatic treatment: Treated with enzyme working solution in the autostainer for 10 min.</p><p>Antigen retrieval-Solution</p><p>0.01 M citrate buffer pH 6: S2031, ChemMate, Target Retrieval Solution, Dako, diluted with distilled water in 1:10.</p><p>EDTA solution: S3307, Antigen Retrieval Reagent, high pH, Dako, diluted with distilled water in 1:10.</p><p>0.01 M citrate buffer pH 8: 0.01 M citrate buffer pH 8 with 0.1% NP-40.</p><p>200 &#x000b5;g/ml proteinase K: 200 &#x000b5;g/ml proteinase K (Code No. 9033, Takara Bio Co.)-0.05 M Tris buffer saline pH 7.2.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3a" position="float"><label>Table&#x000a0;3a</label><caption><p>Immunohistochemical evaluation (Proliferation and stem cells)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr valign="middle"><th rowspan="2" align="center" valign="middle" colspan="1">Lesion examined</th><th rowspan="2" align="center" valign="middle" colspan="1">Proliferation (Ki67 antigen)</th><th colspan="2" align="center" rowspan="1">Stem cells<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CD117</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CD34 (Besides capillary endothelia)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some positive cells in the neck portion (Neck Ki67-positive cells) (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">3</xref>a).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rare small and medium-sized CD117-positive cells in the loose interglandular stroma.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few flat epithelia in the neck portion, some faintly positive glandular cells and a few stromal positive cells (Supersensitive, Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>e).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not examined.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not examined.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not examined.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 1<hr/></td><td rowspan="2" align="left" valign="top" colspan="1">Many neck Ki67-positive cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No CD117-positive cells.<hr/></td><td rowspan="2" align="left" valign="top" colspan="1">Some faintly positive glandular cells (Supersensitive).</td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some CD117-positive cells in the neck portion (Neck CD117-positive cells). A few somewhat-aggregative CD117-positive cells among infiltrates (Aggregative CD117-positive cells) (Supersensitive, Fig,&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>b).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many neck Ki67-positive cells in the elongated neck portion.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few neck CD117-positive cells. A few aggregative CD117-positive cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some faintly positive glandular cells (Supersensitive).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 4</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many neck Ki67-positive cells in the elongated neck portion and a few Ki67-positive cells in the fundic glandular portion (fundic Ki67-positive cells).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Besides the neck CD117-positive cells, some CD117-positive cells in fundic glandular areas (Fundic CD117-positive cells). Some aggregative CD117-positive cells (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>a, c).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many positive glandular cells and stromal positive cells (Supersensitive, Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>f).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 5</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many neck Ki67-positive cells in the elongated neck portion and some fundic Ki67-positive cells (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">3</xref>b).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Besides neck CD117-positive cells, fundic CD117-positive cells and aggregative CD117-positive cells. Some CD117-positive cells around the neck portion (adhesive CD117-positive cells).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Residual 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few cells with faint or granular cytoplasmic stain of CD117 in the stroma.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>g).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some Ki67-positive cells in the deep to base portion of the glands (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">3</xref>c).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many faint-CD117-positive cells in the deep portion. Obviously CD117-positive cells in the base portion. A few cells with faint or granular cytoplasmic stain of CD117 in the stroma (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">5</xref>d).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No positive glandular cells (Supersensitive).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr align="left"><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3b" position="float"><label>Table 3b</label><caption><p>Immunohistochemical evaluation (Programmed cell death)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="2" align="center" valign="middle" colspan="1">Glandular lesion examined</th><th rowspan="2" align="center" valign="middle" colspan="1">Muramidase</th><th rowspan="2" align="center" valign="middle" colspan="1">CD68</th><th colspan="2" align="center" rowspan="1">Programmed cell death<hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">hAR and polymer method of cleaved casapase-3</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">eAR and supersensitive method of beclin-1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Strongly positive surface and neck cells and weakly positive fundic cells. Some positive stromal cells (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">6</xref>a).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some positive cells in the thin stroma in the upper portion and fewer positive cells in the deep portion of the mucosa.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some foveolar positive epithelia below the surface (Foveolar positive cells) (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F7">7</xref>b).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Weakly positive-fundic glandular cells just under the neck portion and nuclear stain in a few cells among them (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F8">8</xref>a).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not examined.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few positive cells in the loose stroma in the deep portion.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Relatively many foveolar positive cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Weakly positive-fundic glandular cells just under the neck portion.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Strongly positive entire glandular cells. Some positive stromal cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many aggregative positive cells among the infiltrates.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few foveolar positive cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above in parts.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few foveolar positive cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above in parts (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F8">8</xref>d).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few foveolar positive cells and a few sloughed positive cells in the glandular lumen.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 4</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not examined.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A few foveolar positive cells and a few positive cells in the neck portion (Neck positive cells).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Very weakly positive fundic glandular cells and some strongly positive fundic cells (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F8">8</xref>c).</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Inflamed 5</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">6</xref>b).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many aggregative positive cells among the infiltrates.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many foveolar positive cells and some neck positive cells (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F7">7</xref>d, e).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above, especially among dense infiltrates.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Residual 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above but many positive stromal cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some positive cells in the stroma.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Positive cells in the stroma.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Some positive fundic glandular cells and nuclear stain in a few cells.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Weakly positive surface cells and strongly positive basal portion cells.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cell apex and supranuclear stain in the surface cells and granular supranuclear stain in the base portion (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">6</xref>c).</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Many positive surface metaplastic epithelia.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Negative.</td></tr><tr><td colspan="5" rowspan="1"><hr/></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metaplastic 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Same as above.</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Negative.</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>